    "def-p4-p4m-lattice-symmetry-groups": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-group",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **group** is a monoid $(G,*,e)$ ([[def-semigroup-and-monoid]]) in which every\nelement is invertible ([[def-invertible-element]]). Written out, a group is a set\n$G$ with a binary operation $*$ ([[def-binary-operation]]) and an element\n$e \\in G$ such that\n\n- **(G1)** $(x * y) * z = x * (y * z)$ for all $x, y, z \\in G$;\n- **(G2)** $e * x = x = x * e$ for every $x \\in G$;\n- **(G3)** for every $x \\in G$ there is $y \\in G$ with $y * x = e = x * y$.\n\nBy [[lem-identity-unique]] the element $e$ of (G2) is unique, and by\n[[lem-inverse-unique]] the element $y$ of (G3) is unique for each $x$ and is\ndetermined by either one of the two equations alone. So the notations $e$ for the\nidentity and $x^{-1}$ for the inverse of $x$ are legitimate, and asserting\n$y * x = e$ alone already says $y = x^{-1}$.\n\nA group is **abelian**, or **commutative**, when its operation is commutative:\n$x * y = y * x$ for all $x, y \\in G$.\n\n**Notation.** The operation is usually written multiplicatively, $xy$ for\n$x * y$, with identity $e$ (or $1$) and inverse $x^{-1}$. For an abelian group it\nis often written additively, $x + y$, with identity $0$ and inverse $-x$; the two\nnotations describe the same notion and nothing below depends on the choice. We\nwrite $G$ for the group when the operation is understood.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-group-action",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group with identity $e$ and let $X$ be a set. A **left action** of\n$G$ on $X$ is a function $G\\times X\\to X$, written $(g,x)\\mapsto g\\cdot x$,\nsuch that\n\n$$e\\cdot x=x,\\qquad (gh)\\cdot x=g\\cdot(h\\cdot x)$$\n\nfor all $g,h\\in G$ and $x\\in X$. Then $X$ is a **$G$-set**. The action is\n**transitive** when every $x,y\\in X$ satisfy $g\\cdot x=y$ for some $g\\in G$.\nIt is **faithful** when $g\\cdot x=x$ for every $x\\in X$ implies $g=e$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "point-groups",
          "claim": "The matrices satisfy $\\rho^4=I$, $\\sigma^2=I$, and $\\sigma\\rho\\sigma=\\rho^{-1}$. The rotation matrices are $I,\\rho,-I,-\\rho$, hence distinct; their products add exponents modulo $4$, and $(\\rho^j)^{-1}=\\rho^{-j}$. The four matrices $\\sigma,\\rho\\sigma,-\\sigma,-\\rho\\sigma$ are distinct, have determinant $-1$, and differ from the rotations, which have determinant $1$. Thus $C_4$ has four elements and $D_4$ has eight. Writing elements of $D_4$ as $\\rho^j\\sigma^\\epsilon$ with $\\epsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}$, the relation $\\sigma\\rho^j=\\rho^{-j}\\sigma$ shows products stay in $D_4$, and $(\\rho^j\\sigma^\\epsilon)^{-1}=\\rho^{-(-1)^\\epsilon j}\\sigma^\\epsilon$ also lies in $D_4$. Both matrix groups preserve $\\mathbb Z^2$ because $\\rho$ and $\\sigma$ are signed permutation matrices.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "affine-groups",
          "claim": "For either matrix group $A$, the pair multiplication is closed on $\\mathbb Z^2\\times A$. It is associative because both parenthesizations of three factors $(t,M),(s,N),(r,Q)$ equal $(t+Ms+MN r,MNQ)$. Its identity is $(0,I)$. For every pair $(t,M)$, the pair $(-M^{-1}t,M^{-1})$ belongs to the same set by 1.1, and $(t,M)(-M^{-1}t,M^{-1})=(0,I)=(-M^{-1}t,M^{-1})(t,M)$. Thus $P_{C_4}$ and $P_{D_4}$ satisfy the group axioms in [F1].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lattice-actions-and-size",
          "claim": "The identity pair acts identically because $(0,I)\\cdot x=x$. The affine maps respect pair multiplication because $M(Nx+s)+t=MNx+Ms+t$, so $((t,M)(s,N))\\cdot x=(t,M)\\cdot((s,N)\\cdot x)$ and [F2] holds. The translations $(t,I)$ are distinct as $t$ ranges over $\\mathbb Z^2$, so each full group is infinite.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The affine pair sets contain the identity pair, so an empty group is outside this definition."
        },
