    "def-pointwise-nonlinearity-and-pooling-on-group-features": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-subgroup",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group ([[def-group]]) with identity $e$. A subset $H \\subseteq G$ is\na **subgroup** of $G$, written $H \\le G$, when\n\n- **(S1)** $e \\in H$;\n- **(S2)** $H$ is closed under the operation: $x, y \\in H$ implies $xy \\in H$\n  ([[def-binary-operation]]);\n- **(S3)** $H$ is closed under inverses: $x \\in H$ implies $x^{-1} \\in H$.\n\nBy (S2) the operation of $G$ restricts to a binary operation on $H$; it is\nassociative there because it is associative on $G$, the element $e$ of (S1) is a\ntwo-sided identity for it ([[def-identity-element]]), and (S3) supplies for each\n$x \\in H$ a two-sided inverse lying in $H$ ([[def-invertible-element]]). So a\nsubgroup, with the restricted operation, is itself a group, and its identity and\nits inverses are those of $G$.\n\nEvery group $G$ has the two **trivial subgroups** $\\{e\\}$ and $G$ itself; a\nsubgroup $H$ with $H \\ne G$ is called **proper**.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-coset",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group and let $H\\le G$ be a subgroup ([[def-group]],\n[[def-subgroup]]). For $g\\in G$, the **left coset** and **right coset** of $H$\nrepresented by $g$ are\n\n$$gH:=\\{gh:h\\in H\\},\\qquad Hg:=\\{hg:h\\in H\\}.$$\n\nThe element $g$ is a **representative** of these cosets. The notation denotes\nsubsets of $G$; it does not assert that either subset is a subgroup.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-group-action",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group with identity $e$ and let $X$ be a set. A **left action** of\n$G$ on $X$ is a function $G\\times X\\to X$, written $(g,x)\\mapsto g\\cdot x$,\nsuch that\n\n$$e\\cdot x=x,\\qquad (gh)\\cdot x=g\\cdot(h\\cdot x)$$\n\nfor all $g,h\\in G$ and $x\\in X$. Then $X$ is a **$G$-set**. The action is\n**transitive** when every $x,y\\in X$ satisfy $g\\cdot x=y$ for some $g\\in G$.\nIt is **faithful** when $g\\cdot x=x$ for every $x\\in X$ implies $g=e$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "coset-action",
          "claim": "If $gH=g'H$, then $g'=g'e\\in g'H=gH$, so [F2] gives $g'=gh_0$ for some $h_0\\in H$. By [F1], $h_0H\\subseteq H$; conversely, every $k\\in H$ equals $h_0(h_0^{-1}k)$ with $h_0^{-1}k\\in H$, so $H\\subseteq h_0H$. Hence $h_0H=H$ and $(ag')H=(ag h_0)H=ag(h_0H)=agH$, proving that $a\\cdot(gH)=(ag)H$ is independent of the representative. It satisfies the identity and composition laws in [F3] because $eg=g$ and $(ab)g=a(bg)$. Thus it is a left action on $G/H$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "coset-pool",
          "claim": "If $g'=gh_0$ with $h_0\\in H$, then $h_0H=H$: closure gives $h_0H\\subseteq H$, and for $k\\in H$, $k=h_0(h_0^{-1}k)\\in h_0H$ by [F1]. Hence $\\{g'h:h\\in H\\}=\\{gh:h\\in H\\}$, so the componentwise maximum defining $Q_HF(gH)$ is independent of the representative.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "operations-defined",
          "claim": "By [F1], $H$ contains the identity, so $H$ and the given set $U$ are nonempty and finite. Each displayed maximum therefore exists in $\\mathbb R$; the pointwise operation and subgroup restriction are also functions on their stated domains. Thus all four operators in the Definition are well-defined.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Neighborhood pooling explicitly requires U finite and nonempty. A subgroup H contains the identity, so H, every coset gH, the group G and its coset set are nonempty; proof step 2.1 therefore takes maxima only over nonempty finite sets. The phrase indexed by names these nonempty domains, not an empty pooling case.",
          "reviewed": {
            "upheld": true,
            "by": "/root/policy_gradient_cpo_repair",
            "reason": "Neighborhood pooling explicitly requires U finite and nonempty. A subgroup H contains the identity, so H, every coset gH, the group G and its coset set are nonempty; proof step 2.1 therefore takes maxima only over nonempty finite sets. The phrase indexed by names these nonempty domains, not an empty pooling case. This explicit hypothesis/outcome is the disposition of the surface detector candidate, not a waiver of a mathematical case.",
            "actual_detector_why": "the item quantifies over a family or indexed aggregate (\\bindexed by\\b)"
          }
        },
