    "thm-group-convolution-equivariance": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-group-action",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group with identity $e$ and let $X$ be a set. A **left action** of\n$G$ on $X$ is a function $G\\times X\\to X$, written $(g,x)\\mapsto g\\cdot x$,\nsuch that\n\n$$e\\cdot x=x,\\qquad (gh)\\cdot x=g\\cdot(h\\cdot x)$$\n\nfor all $g,h\\in G$ and $x\\in X$. Then $X$ is a **$G$-set**. The action is\n**transitive** when every $x,y\\in X$ satisfy $g\\cdot x=y$ for some $g\\in G$.\nIt is **faithful** when $g\\cdot x=x$ for every $x\\in X$ implies $g=e$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-group",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **group** is a monoid $(G,*,e)$ (Semigroup and monoid) in which every\nelement is invertible (Left inverse, right inverse, and invertible element of a monoid). Written out, a group is a set\n$G$ with a binary operation $*$ (Binary operation on a set; associativity, commutativity, and a subset closed under the operation) and an element\n$e \\in G$ such that\n\n- **(G1)** $(x * y) * z = x * (y * z)$ for all $x, y, z \\in G$;\n- **(G2)** $e * x = x = x * e$ for every $x \\in G$;\n- **(G3)** for every $x \\in G$ there is $y \\in G$ with $y * x = e = x * y$.\n\nBy A left identity and a right identity for the same binary operation are equal; hence there is at most one two-sided identity the element $e$ of (G2) is unique, and by\nIn a monoid, a left inverse and a right inverse of the same element are equal; hence an invertible element has exactly one inverse, and it is two-sided the element $y$ of (G3) is unique for each $x$ and is\ndetermined by either one of the two equations alone. So the notations $e$ for the\nidentity and $x^{-1}$ for the inverse of $x$ are legitimate, and asserting\n$y * x = e$ alone already says $y = x^{-1}$.\n\nA group is **abelian**, or **commutative**, when its operation is commutative:\n$x * y = y * x$ for all $x, y \\in G$.\n\n**Notation.** The operation is usually written multiplicatively, $xy$ for\n$x * y$, with identity $e$ (or $1$) and inverse $x^{-1}$. For an abelian group it\nis often written additively, $x + y$, with identity $0$ and inverse $-x$; the two\nnotations describe the same notion and nothing below depends on the choice. We\nwrite $G$ for the group when the operation is understood.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-group-action-on-feature-maps",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group acting on the left on a set $S$, and let $V$ be a set of feature values. A feature map is a function $f:S\\to V$. For $u\\in G$, the induced transformation of feature maps is\n\n$$\n(L_u f)(x)=f(u^{-1}\\cdot x)\\qquad(x\\in S).\n$$\n\nIt is a left action on the set of feature maps: the group acts by reindexing sites and leaves the feature values unchanged. In particular, for maps $F:G\\to V$ on the group itself with its left multiplication action, $(L_uF)(g)=F(u^{-1}g)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-group-convolution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a discrete group acting on the lattice $\\mathbb Z^2$ on the left. Let $C_{\\rm in},C_{\\rm out}$ be positive integers, and regard channel vectors as row vectors. For a feature map $f:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}}$ and a filter $\\Psi:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}\\times C_{\\rm out}}$, the group-to-group cross-correlation is the map $C_\\Psi f:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm out}}$ defined by\n\n$$\n(C_\\Psi f)(g)=\\sum_{h\\in G} f(h)\\Psi(g^{-1}h).\n$$\n\nFor an image feature map $f:\\mathbb Z^2\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}}$ and a lattice filter $\\Psi:\\mathbb Z^2\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}\\times C_{\\rm out}}$, the first-layer image-to-group cross-correlation is the map $E_\\Psi f:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm out}}$ defined by\n\n$$\n(E_\\Psi f)(g)=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathbb Z^2} f(x)\\Psi(g^{-1}\\cdot x).\n$$\n\nEach displayed sum is required to have finite support or to converge absolutely in every output coordinate. The first operation takes group-indexed features and filters; the second takes lattice-indexed image features and filters and returns group-indexed features. These are cross-correlations, with the filter argument displayed as written; they need not be commutative, and no finiteness of $G$ is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-group-convolution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a discrete group acting on the lattice $\\mathbb Z^2$ on the left. Let $C_{\\rm in},C_{\\rm out}$ be positive integers, and regard channel vectors as row vectors. For a feature map $f:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}}$ and a filter $\\Psi:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}\\times C_{\\rm out}}$, the group-to-group cross-correlation is the map $C_\\Psi f:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm out}}$ defined by\n\n$$\n(C_\\Psi f)(g)=\\sum_{h\\in G} f(h)\\Psi(g^{-1}h).\n$$\n\nFor an image feature map $f:\\mathbb Z^2\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}}$ and a lattice filter $\\Psi:\\mathbb Z^2\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}\\times C_{\\rm out}}$, the first-layer image-to-group cross-correlation is the map $E_\\Psi f:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm out}}$ defined by\n\n$$\n(E_\\Psi f)(g)=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathbb Z^2} f(x)\\Psi(g^{-1}\\cdot x).\n$$\n\nEach displayed sum is required to have finite support or to converge absolutely in every output coordinate. The first operation takes group-indexed features and filters; the second takes lattice-indexed image features and filters and returns group-indexed features. These are cross-correlations, with the filter argument displayed as written; they need not be commutative, and no finiteness of $G$ is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-pointwise-nonlinearity-and-pooling-on-group-features",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group and let $F:G\\to\\mathbb R^C$ be a feature map with $C$ channels. A pointwise nonlinearity determined by $\\nu:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ is $N_\\nu F$, where $(N_\\nu F)(g)_c=\\nu(F(g)_c)$. For a nonempty finite set $U\\subseteq G$, non-strided neighborhood max pooling is $P_UF$, where $(P_UF)(g)_c=\\max_{u\\in U}F(gu)_c$; the output is still indexed by $G$. For a subgroup $H\\le G$, subgroup sampling is the restriction $R_HF=F|_H$, indexed by $H$.\n\nThe left-coset set is $G/H=\\{gH:g\\in G\\}$, where $gH=\\{gh:h\\in H\\}$. Its left $G$-action is $a\\cdot(gH)=(ag)H$. For finite $H$, left-coset max pooling is the map $Q_HF:G/H\\to\\mathbb R^C$ given by $(Q_HF)(gH)_c=\\max_{h\\in H}F(gh)_c$. All maxima are taken separately in each channel. When $H=G$ is finite, $G/H$ has one element and $Q_HF$ is a group-invariant summary.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-equivariant-neural-layer",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let a group $G$ act on sets $X$ and $Y$ by left actions $T$ and $T'$, respectively. A layer is a function $\\Phi:X\\to Y$. It is $G$-equivariant when\n\n$$\n\\Phi(T_gx)=T'_g\\Phi(x)\\qquad(g\\in G,\\ x\\in X).\n$$\n\nThe input and output actions need not be the same. The layer is $G$-invariant when $\\Phi(T_gx)=\\Phi(x)$ for every $g\\in G$ and $x\\in X$; equivalently, it is equivariant into $Y$ equipped with the trivial action.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-discrete-convolutional-layer",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $D,O\\subseteq\\mathbb Z^2$ be input and output index sets, let $C_{\\rm in},C_{\\rm out}$ be positive integers, and let $x:D\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}}$ assign a row vector of input channels to each input site. A boundary extension is a function $\\widetilde x:\\mathbb Z^2\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}}$ whose restriction to $D$ is $x$; zero padding is the extension that is zero off $D$. Choose a finite receptive-field offset set $K\\subseteq\\mathbb Z^2$, a positive integer stride vector $s\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2$, an origin $a\\in\\mathbb Z^2$, one matrix $W_\\delta\\in\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}\\times C_{\\rm out}}$ for each $\\delta\\in K$, and an output bias row vector $b\\in\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm out}}$. The discrete convolutional layer is the map $y:O\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm out}}$ given by\n\n$$\ny(p)=b+\\sum_{\\delta\\in K}\\widetilde x(a+s\\odot p+\\delta)W_\\delta\\qquad(p\\in O),\n$$\n\nwhere $s\\odot p$ is coordinatewise multiplication. Thus each output uses only the input sites in its receptive field, mixes input channels by matrix multiplication, and uses the same weights and bias at every output site. With unit stride on the full lattice and no boundary truncation, this formula is a discrete cross-correlation; many machine-learning software packages call it convolution. Mathematical convolution instead reverses the kernel offsets. The choices of $O$, the extension $\\widetilde x$, and the stride specify cropping, padding, and subsampling, and need not preserve a symmetry of the full lattice.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-p4-p4m-lattice-symmetry-groups",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\rho=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ be the quarter-turn matrix and $\\sigma=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$ a reflection matrix. Let $C_4=\\{\\rho^j:j=0,1,2,3\\}$ and $D_4=\\{\\rho^j,\\rho^j\\sigma:j=0,1,2,3\\}$, with matrix multiplication. For $A=C_4$ or $D_4$, define $P_A=\\mathbb Z^2\\times A$ with multiplication\n\n$$\n(t,M)(s,N)=(t+Ms,MN).\n$$\n\nEach pair $(t,M)$ acts on lattice sites by $x\\mapsto Mx+t$. The p4 lattice-symmetry group is $P_{C_4}$, generated by integer translations and quarter-turns. The p4m lattice-symmetry group is $P_{D_4}$, generated by integer translations, quarter-turns, and reflections. The point groups $C_4$ and $D_4$ have four and eight elements, respectively, but both full groups are infinite because they contain every integer translation.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-equivariant-map-of-group-actions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be $G$-sets (Left group actions, transitive actions, and faithful actions). A function\n$f:X\\to Y$ (A function is a relation $f$ with $(a,b) \\in f$ and $(a,c) \\in f$ implying $b = c$; $f : A \\to B$, the value $f(a)$, domain and codomain) is **$G$-equivariant** when\n\n$$f(g\\cdot x)=g\\cdot f(x)$$\n\nfor every $g\\in G$ and $x\\in X$. An **isomorphism of $G$-sets** is an\nequivariant bijection (Injection, surjection, bijection). Two actions are\n**equivariantly isomorphic** when such a bijection exists between their\n$G$-sets.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-subgroup",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group (Group and abelian group) with identity $e$. A subset $H \\subseteq G$ is\na **subgroup** of $G$, written $H \\le G$, when\n\n- **(S1)** $e \\in H$;\n- **(S2)** $H$ is closed under the operation: $x, y \\in H$ implies $xy \\in H$\n  (Binary operation on a set; associativity, commutativity, and a subset closed under the operation);\n- **(S3)** $H$ is closed under inverses: $x \\in H$ implies $x^{-1} \\in H$.\n\nBy (S2) the operation of $G$ restricts to a binary operation on $H$; it is\nassociative there because it is associative on $G$, the element $e$ of (S1) is a\ntwo-sided identity for it (Left identity, right identity, and two-sided identity for a binary operation), and (S3) supplies for each\n$x \\in H$ a two-sided inverse lying in $H$ (Left inverse, right inverse, and invertible element of a monoid). So a\nsubgroup, with the restricted operation, is itself a group, and its identity and\nits inverses are those of $G$.\n\nEvery group $G$ has the two **trivial subgroups** $\\{e\\}$ and $G$ itself; a\nsubgroup $H$ with $H \\ne G$ is called **proper**.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "def-coset",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group and let $H\\le G$ be a subgroup (Group and abelian group,\nSubgroup). For $g\\in G$, the **left coset** and **right coset** of $H$\nrepresented by $g$ are\n\n$$gH:=\\{gh:h\\in H\\},\\qquad Hg:=\\{hg:h\\in H\\}.$$\n\nThe element $g$ is a **representative** of these cosets. The notation denotes\nsubsets of $G$; it does not assert that either subset is a subgroup.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "group-correlation",
          "claim": "For group-to-group correlation, $(C_\\Psi(L_uf))(g)=\\sum_{h\\in G}f(u^{-1}h)\\Psi(g^{-1}h)$. Reindex by the bijection $h=uk$ to obtain $\\sum_{k\\in G}f(k)\\Psi(g^{-1}uk)$. The identity $(u^{-1}g)^{-1}=g^{-1}u$ follows because both products of $u^{-1}g$ and $g^{-1}u$ are $e$ by associativity and the two-sided inverse laws; two two-sided inverses $a,b$ of $z$ coincide since $a=a(zb)=(az)b=b$. Hence the sum is $\\sum_{k\\in G}f(k)\\Psi((u^{-1}g)^{-1}k)=(C_\\Psi f)(u^{-1}g)=(L_u(C_\\Psi f))(g)$. The reindexing is valid for finite support or absolute convergence.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
