          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pointwise-and-neighborhood-pool",
          "claim": "For every channel $c$, $(N_\\nu(L_uF))(g)_c=\\nu(F(u^{-1}g)_c)=(L_u(N_\\nu F))(g)_c$. Likewise, $(P_U(L_uF))(g)_c=\\max_{v\\in U}F(u^{-1}gv)_c=(P_UF)(u^{-1}g)_c=(L_u(P_UF))(g)_c$. Thus both operators commute with the left action.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "subgroup-and-coset-pool",
          "claim": "For $a\\in H$ and $h\\in H$, $(R_H(L_aF))(h)=F(a^{-1}h)=(L_a(R_HF))(h)$ because $a^{-1}h\\in H$ by [F11]; this proves only $H$-equivariance after subgroup restriction. For finite $H$ and arbitrary $a\\in G$, $(Q_H(L_aF))(gH)_c=\\max_{h\\in H}F(a^{-1}gh)_c=(Q_HF)((a^{-1}g)H)_c=(L_a^{G/H}(Q_HF))(gH)_c$, using the left-coset indexing in [F12] and the action stated in [F6]. If $H=G$ is finite then $G/H$ is a singleton, whose action is trivial, so the output is invariant.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
