            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "image-to-group",
          "claim": "For the first-layer operation, $(E_\\Psi(L_uf))(g)=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathbb Z^2}f(u^{-1}\\cdot x)\\Psi(g^{-1}\\cdot x)$. The map $x=u\\cdot y$ is a bijection of $\\mathbb Z^2$ with inverse $y=u^{-1}\\cdot x$ by [F1], so reindexing gives $\\sum_{y\\in\\mathbb Z^2}f(y)\\Psi(g^{-1}\\cdot(u\\cdot y))=\\sum_{y\\in\\mathbb Z^2}f(y)\\Psi((g^{-1}u)\\cdot y)=(E_\\Psi f)(u^{-1}g)=(L_u(E_\\Psi f))(g)$, using the inverse identity in 1.1. Absolute convergence or finite support justifies the reindexing. For $G=\\mathbb Z^2$ acting by translations, $\\Psi(\\delta)=W_\\delta$ with finite support, full-lattice input and output domains, unit stride, and origin $a=0$, setting $x=p+\\delta$ identifies the sum in this formula with the cross-correlation term of the layer in [F8]. For any fixed origin $a$, that sum is instead $(E_\\Psi f)(p+a)$, which remains translation-equivariant because translations of $\\mathbb Z^2$ commute. The layer's output bias is constant across $p$, so the induced translation action fixes it; adding it preserves equivariance of the full layer.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F8",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "stacking-and-scope",
          "claim": "If $\\Phi$ and $\\Psi$ are equivariant maps for compatible input, intermediate, and output actions, then $(\\Psi\\circ\\Phi)(T_gx)=\\Psi(T'_g\\Phi(x))=T''_g(\\Psi\\circ\\Phi)(x)$ by [F7] and [F10]; induction gives equivariance of any finite stack. The reindexings in steps 1.1 and 2.1 require actual group actions on the full index sets, while [F8] permits arbitrary finite-grid boundary and stride choices; therefore those choices are covered only when compatible with the actions. In particular [F9] shows no finite-group assumption was used for p4 or p4m.",
