    "ex-nonstrided-subgroup-and-coset-pooling": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-pointwise-nonlinearity-and-pooling-on-group-features",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group and let $F:G\\to\\mathbb R^C$ be a feature map with $C$ channels. A pointwise nonlinearity determined by $\\nu:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ is $N_\\nu F$, where $(N_\\nu F)(g)_c=\\nu(F(g)_c)$. For a nonempty finite set $U\\subseteq G$, non-strided neighborhood max pooling is $P_UF$, where $(P_UF)(g)_c=\\max_{u\\in U}F(gu)_c$; the output is still indexed by $G$. For a subgroup $H\\le G$, subgroup sampling is the restriction $R_HF=F|_H$, indexed by $H$.\n\nThe left-coset set is $G/H=\\{gH:g\\in G\\}$, where $gH=\\{gh:h\\in H\\}$. Its left $G$-action is $a\\cdot(gH)=(ag)H$. For finite $H$, left-coset max pooling is the map $Q_HF:G/H\\to\\mathbb R^C$ given by $(Q_HF)(gH)_c=\\max_{h\\in H}F(gh)_c$. All maxima are taken separately in each channel. When $H=G$ is finite, $G/H$ has one element and $Q_HF$ is a group-invariant summary.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-p4-p4m-lattice-symmetry-groups",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\rho=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ be the quarter-turn matrix and $\\sigma=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$ a reflection matrix. Let $C_4=\\{\\rho^j:j=0,1,2,3\\}$ and $D_4=\\{\\rho^j,\\rho^j\\sigma:j=0,1,2,3\\}$, with matrix multiplication. For $A=C_4$ or $D_4$, define $P_A=\\mathbb Z^2\\times A$ with multiplication\n\n$$\n(t,M)(s,N)=(t+Ms,MN).\n$$\n\nEach pair $(t,M)$ acts on lattice sites by $x\\mapsto Mx+t$. The p4 lattice-symmetry group is $P_{C_4}$, generated by integer translations and quarter-turns. The p4m lattice-symmetry group is $P_{D_4}$, generated by integer translations, quarter-turns, and reflections. The point groups $C_4$ and $D_4$ have four and eight elements, respectively, but both full groups are infinite because they contain every integer translation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-group-convolution-equivariance",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a discrete group acting on the left on the square lattice $\\mathbb Z^2$, and let feature maps take values in finite-dimensional row-vector channel spaces. Assume every group or lattice sum below has finite support or converges absolutely in each output coordinate. For group-indexed features $f:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}}$ and a matrix-valued filter $\\Psi:G\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}\\times C_{\\rm out}}$, group-to-group cross-correlation satisfies\n\n$$\nC_\\Psi(L_u f)=L_u(C_\\Psi f)\\qquad(u\\in G).\n$$\n\nFor an image feature map $f:\\mathbb Z^2\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}}$ and a lattice filter $\\Psi:\\mathbb Z^2\\to\\mathbb R^{C_{\\rm in}\\times C_{\\rm out}}$, the first-layer image-to-group cross-correlation also satisfies\n\n$$\nE_\\Psi(L_u f)=L_u(E_\\Psi f)\\qquad(u\\in G),\n$$\n\nwhere the input action is induced by the action on $\\mathbb Z^2$ and the output action is the left action on maps on $G$. The bias-free sum in the ordinary full-lattice, unit-stride discrete layer with origin $a=0$ is the translation-group instance of this formula; any fixed origin shift and the layer's constant output bias preserve translation equivariance. Componentwise pointwise nonlinearities and finite-neighborhood max pooling without subsampling commute with the left action. Restricting a group feature map to a subgroup $H\\le G$ preserves $H$-equivariance; for finite $H$, left-coset max pooling produces a $G$-equivariant feature map on $G/H$, and when $H=G$ is finite it is invariant. Finite compositions of equivariant layers remain equivariant.\n\nThe full p4 and p4m lattice symmetry groups are infinite because they contain all integer translations; no finiteness assumption on $G$ is made. On a finite grid, the theorem applies only when the domain, output sampling, and boundary convention respect the stated actions. Arbitrary cropping, padding, or stride need not do so. No continuous-rotation, universal-approximation, or empirical-superiority claim follows.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-subgroup",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group ([[def-group]]) with identity $e$. A subset $H \\subseteq G$ is\na **subgroup** of $G$, written $H \\le G$, when\n\n- **(S1)** $e \\in H$;\n- **(S2)** $H$ is closed under the operation: $x, y \\in H$ implies $xy \\in H$\n  ([[def-binary-operation]]);\n- **(S3)** $H$ is closed under inverses: $x \\in H$ implies $x^{-1} \\in H$.\n\nBy (S2) the operation of $G$ restricts to a binary operation on $H$; it is\nassociative there because it is associative on $G$, the element $e$ of (S1) is a\ntwo-sided identity for it ([[def-identity-element]]), and (S3) supplies for each\n$x \\in H$ a two-sided inverse lying in $H$ ([[def-invertible-element]]). So a\nsubgroup, with the restricted operation, is itself a group, and its identity and\nits inverses are those of $G$.\n\nEvery group $G$ has the two **trivial subgroups** $\\{e\\}$ and $G$ itself; a\nsubgroup $H$ with $H \\ne G$ is called **proper**.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
